-{"status": "pending", "primary_model": "glm-5.3-flash", "primary_provider": "zai-glm-default", "created_at": "2026-09-21T11:23:44+00:00", "attempts": 0, "id": "TJ-GAP-070", "priority": "P1", "title": "[P1] Engine crash inside a user rule file fails the firewall OPEN: bridge catches the exception and emits action=allow with a [bridge-error] reason the wrapper never inspects — one malformed user rule (e.g. priority: not-a-number) silently disables ALL rule protection", "detail": "Verified end-to-end 2026-09-21: with TERMINAL_JAIL_INTERRUPTOR_RULES_DIR pointing at a dir whose only yaml sets priority: not-a-number, `bridge({'command': 'true'})` returns rc=0, action=allow, rule_id=null, reason '[bridge-error] interrupt() raised an exception — fail-open: allowing command' (interruptor_bridge.py:68-71 catches Exception and calls _emit_fail_open), and the full wrapper `standalone/terminal-jail true` exits 0 and RUNS the command with zero warning — enforce mode's documented fail-closed contract does not trigger because the bridge exits 0 with valid JSON. Two wrapper fail-open hazards sit beside it: the verdict parser does `|| echo \"allow\"` on any bridge stdout that is not JSON (standalone/terminal-jail:319), and empty-stdout-with-exit-0 falls through unparsed. Control probe (clean rules dir): builtin-chmod-777-root still blocks — the firewall itself is healthy; the hole is exception-to-allow. DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-6 fixed the INPUT schema boundary (bridge still fails open there, documented); TJ-GAP-063 fixed the BRIDGE-UNAVAILABLE boundary (fails closed); the ENGINE-EXCEPTION boundary was never decided and currently defaults to allow.", "reasoning": "stand-in PM cycle 2026-09-21 (hunter-verified real-use finding; PM re-ran every load-bearing probe before gating)", "review_notes": "Decide and implement the contract explicitly: (a) preferred — the bridge distinguishes 'policy says allow' from 'engine could not evaluate': on exception emit action=deny (or a dedicated 'error' action) with the [bridge-error] reason so the wrapper's enforce mode blocks, keeping the allow envelope ONLY for malformed stdin/transport where blocking could brick the host shell; validate rules at LOAD time too (rule-pack-tool-style schema pass over rules.d on RuleSet init: refuse the whole file with a loud one-line stderr note, so a typo cannot arm this path). (b) wrapper-side hardening regardless: treat any verdict carrying a non-empty reason starting with [bridge-error] as block in enforce mode, and treat empty/non-JSON bridge stdout as block (never `|| echo allow`). PASS: (1) poison-priority fixture (priority: not-a-number) -> `standalone/terminal-jail true` exits 126 with a block/error box and the payload does not run, enforce mode; (2) the same fixture in warn mode runs with a loud warning line; (3) bridge emitting garbage on stdout -> enforce blocks; (4) all existing suites stay green and the clean-rules control probe still allows/blocks exactly as before (no regression on the DF-6 contract).", "source": "pm-standin-2026-09-21"}
